[Music]  the job of being a hedge fund manager is  often met with a lot of controversy it  seems like their jobs are not only  boring but also lack meaning they  generally work 80 to 100 hours per week  for decades just to make their clients  richer and then there's also the whole  manipulation side of things that many  despise even some of the highest profile  figures in the world like elon musk have  knocked down the idea of being a fund  manager but despite the criticism hedge  fund managers are some of the highest  paid individuals in the world often  earning hundreds of millions if not  billions per year and the man who topped  the list in 2020 was a man named izzy  englander who pulled in a whopping 3.8  billion dollars his flagship fund was up  26 last year which was their strongest  performance in 20 years meanwhile second  place went to the famous jim simons who  earned over a billion dollars less at  2.6 billion so here's how easy englander  beat out even the most iconic managers  in the world and became the world's  highest paid hedge fund manager  taking a look back izzy was born in 1948  in brooklyn to a jewish migrant family  his parents were originally from poland  but they moved to the us in 1947 after  they were extradited to a soviet labor  camp following world war ii you could  say that his parents left everything  behind to come to the u.s but the sad  truth was that there was nothing left  for them in poland in the first place  all of izzy's father's family and much  of his mother's family were killed  during the holocaust despite all the  pain and discrimination the family had  gone through because of their religion  the family remained to devout jews and  that's still the case with izzy to this  day  anyway izzy developed a strong interest  in the stock market from a rather young  age by the time he was in high school he  was already trading stocks in his free  time daisy would go on to attend a new  york university where he majored in  finance and after graduation in 1970 he  found a job at the wall street firm  kaufman alzberg and co  his job at kaufman consisted of trading  convertible securities and options which  were new financial instruments at the  time  as many of y'all probably know options  are extremely volatile and can move  hundreds of percent in a day so this job  really taught izzy how to be extremely  disciplined and to manage risk in the  meantime izzy enrolled in nyu's mba  program and attended classes in the  evenings but he never ended up  completing the program as he would get  distracted by an opportunity  you see around the same time the  american stock exchange also began to  list options and izzy would go ahead and  buy a seat on the exchange this allowed  izzy to create his own floor brokerage  house called ia englander and koa and  that brings us into izzy's first  business  ia englander and co was just a simple  brokerage that allowed customers to buy  and sell stocks and bonds while it was  nothing groundbreaking the company was  profitable and it's actually still  around to this day izzy still even holds  his stake in the company but he's not  involved in day-to-day operations anyway  after running this company for several  years izzy would start an investment  firm called the jaime securities and co  with a man named john mohren jr in 1985  but this business wasn't nearly as  smooth as the first  in the beginning though it looked like  things were going great as the bellsburg  family would invest 75 million dollars  into the company the problem though was  that jon had a dark past that would come  to haunt him before opening the business  with izzy john used to work for a man  named ivan bowsky who would end up being  convicted for insider trading  boski said he would plead guilty and  would cooperate with federal authorities  in a widening investigation of insider  trading ivan would testify against john  in return for a reduced sentence and  john would end up being convicted for  aiding in insider trading izzy wasn't  associated with this incident but given  all of the bad press jon had garnered  for the company izzy had no choice but  to shut it down  by this point izzy had become quite  wealthy thanks to his first two  businesses and he would use this money  to start up millennium management with  ronald shearer in 1989. izzy put in five  million dollars of his own money and the  bellsburg family would put in another  two million dollars once again though  the company would get off to a rough  start and ronald would leave the company  just one year later in 1990 but izzy  wasn't willing to start over again so he  would continue nonetheless  in terms of growing millennium  management and generating profits gazia  leveraged three key strategies with the  first being convertible arbitrage a  convertible security is usually a  corporate bond or preferred stock of a  given company that can be converted into  regular stock with a convertible  arbitrage fund managers are looking for  discrepancies in price between  convertible securities and common stock  for example maybe tesla preferred stock  is trading at one thousand dollars while  the common stock is trading at one  thousand and five dollars the idea is  that the gap will close and that both  securities will eventually trade for the  same price in such a scenario the  funding manager will buy the preferred  stock at one thousand dollars and short  the common stock at one thousand and  five dollars if you look closely you'll  notice that this leaves the manager with  no directional bias  it doesn't matter if tesla doubles  tomorrow or if it crashes eighty percent  as long as the discrepancy between the  preferred and common stock remains five  dollars the manager loses no money by  eliminating directional bias managers  are able to heavily reduce risk but that  doesn't mean that there's no risk if the  discrepancy between the two securities  grows you start to lose money fast as  you're bleeding money from both sides  this is extremely rare but it has  happened before  one example of this was with the gm  stock in 2005. at the time many hedge  funds had long positions in gm  convertible bonds and short positions in  gm stock but suddenly gm stock was  downgraded which caused their  convertible bonds to crash in value  you would think that the stock would  also follow suit but a billionaire  investor was actively buying the dip  which propped up the stock price and  made the disparity explode so clearly  things can go bad but in most cases  convertible arbitrage is an easy way to  capitalize on pricing discrepancies with  low risk moving on to easy second  strategy we have statistical arbitrage  similar to convertible arbitrage  statistical arbitrage aims to leverage  price discrepancies this time the goal  is to leverage the discrepancy between  the market and an individual stock or  industry unless a stock receives some  crazy news or has a killer earnings  report it follows the overall market now  a stock may be more volatile than the  market but this can also be measured by  something called beta for example for  every one percent the s p 500 rises you  may be able to expect tesla to rise five  percent barring any elon tweets but what  if the market is up one percent and  tesla is also only up one percent  in this scenario it's statistically  likely that tesla will rise another four  percent so it would be a relatively safe  bet to buy tesla stock  similarly if the market is only up one  percent but tesla is up nine percent  it's statistically likely that tesla  will drop four percent and it would be a  safe fed to short tesla stock  statistical arbitrage looks to leverage  such over and under performances of the  market to squeeze out some profits and  finally moving on to ac's third key  strategy we have fundamental long and  short pairing this is really the bread  and butter of all hedge funds you go  ahead and buy some stocks and short some  other stocks at the same time this  reduces your overall exposure to the  market some funds employ neutral  strategies where they have an equal  amount in long and short positions while  other funds utilize long biases which  means that they have more and long  positions there's really nothing unique  or special about this strategy but being  a hedge fund izzy often finds themselves  leveraging this strategy as well  [Music]  in terms of trading philosophies izzy  only has a few simple guidelines but  these have proven extremely valuable for  him his first rule for trading is  maintaining severe risk control when  you're playing with tens of billions of  dollars this should really be a given  but it's not uncommon to hear about  hedge fund managers taking outsized bets  and getting destroyed so clearly  sticking to risk guidelines is not as  easy as it sounds moving on to ac second  rule we have be committed to  non-directional strategies as we saw  with all of these trading strategies  none of them depended on the market  going up or down in fact the direction  of the market had no impact on izzy's  performance and this is extremely  important because the hardest part isn't  making money in a bull market or a bear  market but making money in a sideways  market and finally moving on to ac's  last rule we have a warren buffett type  role which is never forget to observe  their first two rules  applying these strategies and  philosophies over 30 years has allowed  millennium management to rock it from 35  million in 1999 to 57 billion assets  today making them one of the largest  hedge funds in the world  they currently operate a total of 200  different investment teams and employ 2  800 people if you're thinking about  getting a job here though you better be  extremely tough as employment and  compensation are strictly dependent on  individual quarterly performance  millennium management along with the  rest of wall street has received massive  criticism for these high-pressure work  environments but despite how the media  paints them people continue to flock  towards these companies because the  upside potential is extraordinary as for  easy englander today he spends a lot of  his effort on supporting the jewish  community as much as possible since 2006  the englander foundation has donated a  total of 20 million dollars most of  which has gone to jewish schools and  organizations izzy hasn't been shy and  splurging on himself either in 2014 for  instance he bought a duplex in new  york's park avenue for a whopping 71.3  million dollars which is a record price  for a manhattan co-op at the end of the  day izzy's life matches the hedge fund  stereotype pretty well he's made his  fortune on leveraging municipal price  discrepancies in the market but despite  making so much money it's not clear what  sort of legacy and impact millennium  really has on the world you could argue  that izzy is just really good at a  zero-sum game but that he's not really  creating any real value regardless of  these critiques though it seems like  izzy is enjoying his life just fine and  in the end that's all that really  matters would you guys consider becoming  a hedge fund manager comment that down  below also drop a like if you wish you  had a billion dollar salary and of  course consider joining our discord  community to suggest future video ideas  and consider subscribing and see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys

